# Retrospective notes

Lessons live in `.cursor/project-memory/lessons/` as `YYYY-MM-DD-short-slug.md`, written
in the project's **communication language** (see `project/stack.mdc`). Section titles in
those folders must match that language — if you still see English stubs in a `ru`
project, replace them from `docs/ru/project-memory/` (onboarding Step 3).

## When to write one

- A bug or regression was fixed and the cause was not obvious.
- An assumption about the codebase turned out wrong and cost a rewrite.
- A review or the user corrected an approach in a way that will recur.
- A non-obvious environment or tooling trap cost real time.

## When not to

Typos, one-off mistakes with no pattern, anything already covered by an existing rule, or
a note that only restates the diff. Noise here is worse than nothing — every entry is
something a future agent may read.

## Format

Keep it under 20 lines. Facts, not narrative.

```markdown
# <short title>

- **Symptom:** what was observed.
- **Cause:** the actual root cause, not the first suspect.
- **Fix:** what was changed, with the file paths.
- **Rule of thumb:** the one sentence a future agent needs.
- **Scope:** where this applies (module, layer, whole repo).
```

## Promotion

- Three or more entries pointing at the same rule of thumb → propose a project rule
  instead of a fourth note.
- A promoted rule becomes a small file in `.cursor/rules/project/` with a precise
  `description` and `globs`, and `alwaysApply: false` unless it is a true always-on
  prohibition. Adding to the always-on set is a deliberate cost — say so when proposing.
- When a lesson is promoted, link the rule from the lesson entry and stop duplicating it.
- Never edit `constitution.mdc` to add project specifics. It stays portable.
